WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.160
The MacBook Pro and 5K iMac have now been
updated for 2019, and we’ve just finished

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.650
testing them, so let’s talk about what’s
changed since last year and compare performance

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.780
to help you decide which Mac you should buy.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:16.280
Here are the specs for the models of each
Mac we tested, both pretty much specced out

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.560
except that we didn’t pay the extra $200
for the better 8-core processor on the MacBook

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.680
Pro because you only get around 2% faster
performance, which is not worth it at all.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.699
And for the iMac, we paid only $300 for 64GB
of RAM by upgrading it ourselves instead of

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:37.069
paying Apple $1,000 for that same upgrade,
and it was incredibly easy and simple to do.

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:40.949
You can check out our RAM upgrade tutorial
video by clicking the card above, and we’ll

00:00:40.949 --> 00:00:42.989
link the RAM we used down in the description.

00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:46.719
We already expect the iMac to perform better
than the MacBook Pro, but the main point of

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:50.680
this video is to see if the difference is
small enough to make the portability of the

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.210
MacBook Pro worth it over being tied down
to a desktop iMac.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:59.159
For the new 15” MacBook Pro, not much has
changed except for the updated butterfly keyboard

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:03.530
which has new materials for the switches which
should hopefully fix the keyboard issues once

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.559
and for all.

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:09.020
Not only that, but we noticed much better
temps across the board and quieter fans compared

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.950
to the 2018 i9 model which suffered from a
ton of thermal throttling.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.250
To learn more about it, watch our 2019 MacBook
Pro review.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:20.670
For the 5K iMac, nothing at all has changed
on the exterior, but on the inside, we get

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:26.270
a new Vega 48 graphics option which is much
more powerful than the old Radeon Pro 580.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:30.100
Not only that, but the new 8-core performs
extremely well and the fans are noticeably

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:36.020
more quiet compared to the 2017 iMac, despite
the old model having only 4 cores.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.939
Now let’s jump into performance.

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:42.850
Starting with Geekbench 4, we see around 10%
faster multi-core speeds, which isn’t much,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:48.220
but in the more realistic Cinebench R20 test,
we got around 30% higher scores on the iMac,

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:52.920
and that’s because of the MacBook Pros thermal
limitations, so this shows a good representation

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.040
of performance differences in processor-heavy
tasks like photo editing, audio production

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.170
and programming.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.940
Now testing raw graphics performance in Geekbench
4, we’re getting about 2x more performance

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.960
on the iMac, which will give you a good indication
of performance differences in apps that use

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.350
a lot of graphics, like video editing, 3D
rendering and animation.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:16.700
For gaming performance, we tested Unigine’s
Heaven benchmark, which gives a good idea

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.300
of the differences you’ll see while gaming
and the iMac got 80% more FPS thanks to that

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.780
Vega 48 chip, and if you want to see it in
action, click the card above for our 5K iMac

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:26.780
gaming video.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.410
Now for photo editors, let’s run some tests
in the latest version of Lightroom.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:36.800
Importing 50 42MP RAW photos, they both only
took around 5 seconds, so no difference there.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.920
Converting them to 1:1 Smart Previews, the
iMac was a few seconds faster, but they’re

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.510
both so quick that it doesn’t make that
big of a difference.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.560
And finally, exporting them all to JPEG, the
iMac was just a little bit faster than the

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.880
MacBook Pro, despite the 30% faster processor
performance in Cinebench R20 and the fact

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.720
that it has 64GB of RAM compared to 32, so
we’re really impressed with the MacBook

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.550
Pros performance for photo editing.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:05.180
Now moving onto video editing in Final Cut
Pro, the iMac is over twice as fast in the

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:08.950
BruceX benchmark because it pretty much just
uses the graphics.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:14.930
Now stabilizing a 20s 4K clip, we get around
40% faster performance with the iMac, but

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:17.720
they’re both so quick that you’ll hardly
notice the difference.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:23.180
Moving onto exporting a 4K H.264 video, which
most YouTubers shoot in this format, we actually

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.430
don’t get any real performance advantage
at all because both machines are limited by

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:32.180
the same Intel Quicksync exporting chip, so
the extra performance of the iMac isn’t

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:33.180
really being used.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.180
And editing smoothness is also identical because
of how easy this 4K footage is to playback.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:39.540
Now here’s a shocker, if we switch to 8
bit 4K H.265 or HEVC footage, the MacBook

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:44.290
Pro is actually more than 2x faster, and that’s
because it packs Apple’s T2 Chip, which

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.730
features hardware HEVC encoding.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:50.180
The new 2019 iMac doesn’t have that chip,
so it takes much longer.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.040
And finally, for the more advanced video editors
out there who edit RAW video, the iMac starts

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:01.570
to shine with about 2x faster performance
exporting a 4K 60 C200 RAW clip, because there’s

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:05.380
no specific chip like Quicksync or the T2
chip that decodes it.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.500
It fully depends on both the graphics and
processor performance for exporting, and that’s

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.400
why we see such a huge difference.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.370
Not only that, but timeline smoothness is
doubled, with the iMac playing it back at

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:19.310
the full 60 frames per second, compared to
30 on the MacBook Pro.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:23.410
So let’s answer the original question, are
you sacrificing a lot of performance by going

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.680
with the portable MacBook Pro instead of the
more powerful iMac?

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.810
Well, for most people, no.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.750
Both of them are so powerful that the difference
won’t really matter that much in the real

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:35.040
world unless you’re someone who deals with
RAW footage or likes to play games on their

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.040
Mac.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:39.760
For common video and photo editing, there’s
really no difference at all, and both displays

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.720
are also equally color accurate as well.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:48.150
Now a plus for the iMac is that you can go
from 8GB to 64GB of RAM for just $300 by following

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:54.510
our RAM upgrade guide, while it costs $400
just to go from 16GB to 32 on the MacBook

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:55.510
Pro.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:59.620
You can even get up to 128GB of RAM on the
iMac for the same price that Apple charges

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:06.090
for 64GB, and yes it does support 128GB, so
if you’re doing virtualization or any task

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.760
that eats up a ton RAM, the iMac is the way
to go.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.780
But for most people, we think the better choice
is the MacBook Pro because we no longer have

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:17.210
the serious thermal throttling issues from
last year, and the portability outweighs any

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.830
extra performance of the iMac in most cases.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.530
If this video helped you out, tap the like
button and click the circle above to subscribe.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:28.190
And check out our iMac RAM upgrade guide right
there, as well as the 2019 MacBook Pro review.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:33.650
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next video!

